/* First Light: the teachings of quran. Generated by .scripts/plans/build-plans.js from
   .scripts/plans/teachings/quran.json; do not edit by hand. */
FLTextPut("teachings", "quran", {"plan":"quran","div":"5a48e62cd7","days":[{"d":1,"at":[4],"ref":"Al-Fatiha 1:5","key":"Thee (alone) we worship; Thee (alone) we ask for help.","s":"Midway through the opening surah, the praise of God turns into direct address; al-Tabari, on 1:5, reads the worship as humbling and abasing oneself before Him alone, preferring this to hope and fear because to the Arabs servitude is rooted in lowliness.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":2,"at":[121],"ref":"Al-Baqarah 2:115","key":"Unto Allah belong the East and the West, and whithersoever ye turn, there is Allah’s Countenance.","s":"Following a warning against barring worshippers from God’s sanctuaries, the surah claims every direction for Him; al-Tabari, on 2:115, reads the naming of East and West as teaching believers that all creatures between them are His and owe Him obedience, facing wherever He directs them.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":3,"at":[196],"ref":"Al-Baqarah 2:190","key":"Fight in the way of Allah against those who fight against you, but begin not hostilities. Lo! Allah loveth not aggressors.","s":"The verse opens the ayahs commanding the believers to fight, slay and drive out those who fight them; al-Tabari, on 2:190, takes it as unrepealed, binding against the disbelievers’ fighting men, and reads the forbidden aggression as killing women, children or those who pay tribute.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":4,"at":[222],"ref":"Al-Baqarah 2:216","key":"Warfare is ordained for you, though it is hateful unto you; but it may happen that ye hate a thing which is good for you, and it may happen that ye love a thing which is bad for you. Allah knoweth, ye know not.","s":"Amid questions put to the Prophet, the believers are told that war is laid on them; al-Tabari, on 2:216, takes the duty as lifted from the rest once enough take it up, since those who stay behind are also promised good.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":5,"at":[292],"ref":"Al-Baqarah 2:286","key":"Allah tasketh not a soul beyond its scope.","s":"Near the surah’s close, after the warning that hidden thoughts will be reckoned, comes this assurance; al-Tabari, on 2:286, reads it as God never overtaxing a soul in its religion, nor holding it to account for an intention, a whispered suggestion or a passing thought.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":6,"at":[323],"ref":"Al 'Imran 3:31","key":"Say, (O Muhammad, to mankind): If ye love Allah, follow me; Allah will love you and forgive you your sins.","s":"Before the surah turns to the family of ’Imran, the Prophet is told what to say to mankind; Ibn Kathir, on 3:31 to 3:32, reads the promise as giving more than was sought, God’s love for them, greater than their love for Him.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":7,"at":[384],"ref":"Al 'Imran 3:92","key":"Ye will not attain unto piety until ye spend of that which ye love.","s":"After a ransom of gold is refused to those dying in disbelief, the surah turns to giving; al-Tabari, on 3:92, reads the piety as God’s kindness in bringing the believers into His Garden, reached by giving from the wealth they most wish to keep.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":8,"at":[451],"ref":"Al 'Imran 3:159","key":"So pardon them and ask forgiveness for them and consult with them upon the conduct of affairs.","s":"Once those who turned back in battle are forgiven, the Prophet is told how to treat them; al-Tabari, on 3:159, reads the command to consult as winning over those not yet firm in Islam and teaching his community to take counsel together after him.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":9,"at":[493],"ref":"An-Nisa 4:1","key":"O mankind! Be careful of your duty to your Lord Who created you from a single soul and from it created its mate and from them twain hath spread abroad a multitude of men and women.","s":"Opening a chapter of laws on orphans, inheritance, marriage and lewdness, the verse addresses all people; al-Tabari, on 4:1, reads it as teaching that all are children of one father and mother, owing each other a brother’s right, the strong giving the weak their due.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":10,"at":[550],"ref":"An-Nisa 4:58","key":"Lo! Allah commandeth you that ye restore deposits to their owners, and, if ye judge between mankind, that ye judge justly.","s":"After a curse on those given a portion of the Scripture who favour idolaters, the passage turns to trusts and judgement; al-Tabari, on 4:58, reads it as spoken to rulers, the shepherd charged with his flock as the next verse charges the flock with obedience.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":11,"at":[627],"ref":"An-Nisa 4:135","key":"O ye who believe! Be ye staunch in justice, witnesses for Allah, even though it be against yourselves or (your) parents or (your) kindred, whether (the case be of) a rich man or a poor man, for Allah is nearer unto both (them ye are).","s":"After rulings on women and orphans, the believers are charged to bear true witness; al-Tabari, on 4:135, reads it as schooling them against the false vouching given for the Banu Ubayriq over their theft, so that no wealth, poverty or kinship bends a witness.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":12,"at":[700],"ref":"Al-Ma'idah 5:32","key":"For that cause We decreed for the Children of Israel that whosoever killeth a human being for other than manslaughter or corruption in the earth, it shall be as if he had killed all mankind, and whoso saveth the life of one, it shall be as if he had saved the life of all mankind.","s":"The tale of Adam’s two sons ends in this decree, before the penalty for waging war on God and His messenger; al-Tabari, on 5:32, reads saving a life as refraining from a killing God forbids, which leaves all people safe from the one who refrains.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":13,"at":[755],"ref":"Al-Ma'idah 5:87","key":"O ye who believe! Forbid not the good things which Allah hath made lawful for you, and transgress not, Lo! Allah loveth not transgressors.","s":"As rulings on food, oaths and strong drink begin, the believers are addressed; al-Tabari, on 5:87, takes ‘transgress not’ to bar any overstepping of God’s bounds, the companions who resolved to forbid themselves what He allowed being reproached for just that.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":14,"at":[773],"ref":"Al-Ma'idah 5:105","key":"O ye who believe! Ye have charge of your own souls. He who erreth cannot injure you if ye are rightly guided.","s":"After those content with their fathers’ ways are answered, the believers are addressed; Ibn Kathir, on 5:105, reads it as a charge to mend their own souls, and not as leave to stop enjoining right and forbidding wrong while that remains possible.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":15,"at":[896],"ref":"Al-An'am 6:108","key":"Revile not those unto whom they pray beside Allah lest they wrongfully revile Allah through ignorance.","s":"After the Prophet is told he is no keeper over the idolaters, the believers are addressed; Ibn Kathir, on 6:108, reads it as a benefit given up for fear of a greater harm, since reviling the idolaters’ gods brings God reviled in return.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":16,"at":[940],"ref":"Al-An'am 6:152","key":"Give full measure and full weight, in justice. We task not any soul beyond its scope.","s":"Among the sacred duties the Prophet is told to recite, the rule of fair trade follows the one on the orphan’s wealth; al-Tabari, on 6:152, reads ‘beyond its scope’ as asking no payer for more than the due, and no payee to settle for less.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":17,"at":[979],"ref":"Al-A'raf 7:26","key":"O Children of Adam! We have revealed unto you raiment to conceal your shame, and splendid vesture, but the raiment of restraint from evil, that is best.","s":"After Adam and his wife leave the Garden, their shame laid bare, the surah turns to his children; al-Tabari, on 7:26, reads the ‘raiment of restraint’ as the fear of God worn next to the soul, gathering faith, good works, modesty and awe.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":18,"at":[1096],"ref":"Al-A'raf 7:143","key":"And when he woke he said: Glory unto Thee! I turn unto Thee repentant, and I am the first of (true) believers.","s":"Moses, after asking to gaze upon his Lord, falls senseless as the mountain is levelled; al-Tabari, on 7:143, reads his repentance as turning from that request, and takes him as first of his people to believe none sees Him in this world and lives.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":19,"at":[1158],"ref":"Al-A'raf 7:205","key":"And do thou (O Muhammad) remember thy Lord within thyself humbly and with awe, below thy breath, at morn and evening. And be not thou of the neglectful.","s":"After the command to heed the recited Qur’an, the chapter’s closing verses turn to private devotion; Ibn Kathir, on 7:205 to 7:206, reads it as urging much remembrance at the day’s start and end, a Meccan command older than the five daily prayers.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":20,"at":[1220],"ref":"Al-Anfal 8:61","key":"And if they incline to peace, incline thou also to it, and trust in Allah. Lo! He, even He, is the Hearer, the Knower.","s":"Following the command to muster force against the enemy, the Prophet is told how to answer offers of truce; al-Tabari, on 8:61, takes it as unrepealed, concerning Qurayza, people of Scripture who may have peace for tribute, while At-Tawbah’s order to slay means Arab idol-worshippers.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":21,"at":[1240],"ref":"At-Tawbah 9:6","key":"And if anyone of the idolaters seeketh thy protection (O Muhammad), then protect him so that he may hear the Word of Allah, and afterward convey him to his place of safety. That is because they are a folk who know not.","s":"After the order to slay the idolaters, the Prophet is told what to do when one of them asks for protection; al-Tabari, on 9:6, reads it as safety while he hears the Qur’an and, if he refuses Islam, safe passage home to his people.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":22,"at":[1356],"ref":"At-Tawbah 9:122","key":"And the believers should not all go out to fight. Of every troop of them, a party only should go forth, that they (who are left behind) may gain sound knowledge in religion, and that they may warn their folk when they return to them, so that they may beware.","s":"In a chapter that commands fighting and condemns staying behind, the believers learn who must march; al-Tabari, on 9:122, takes it of detachments sent while the Prophet stays, and the understanding as gained by the marching party, not those who stay, from seeing God’s victory.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":23,"at":[1421],"ref":"Yunus 10:58","key":"Say: In the bounty of Allah and in His mercy: therein let them rejoice. It is better than what they hoard.","s":"Just after an exhortation and a balm for the breasts are announced to mankind, the Prophet is told what to say; al-Tabari, on 10:58, reads the bounty as Islam and the mercy as the Qur’an, together worth more than this world’s wealth and treasure.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":24,"at":[1461],"ref":"Yunus 10:98","key":"When they believed We drew off from them the torment of disgrace in the life of the world and gave them comfort for a while.","s":"After Pharaoh’s belief while drowning is rebuked, the chapter singles out the folk of Jonah; al-Tabari, on 10:98, takes them as the one people whose faith availed them after the punishment had come down, as Pharaoh’s did not, spared to live out their term.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":25,"at":[1518],"ref":"Hud 11:46","key":"He said: O Noah! Lo! he is not of thy household; lo! he is of evil conduct, so ask not of Me that whereof thou hast no knowledge.","s":"After the flood subsides, Noah pleads that his drowned son belonged to his household; al-Tabari, on 11:46, takes the son as truly his, yet not of the household promised rescue, since he opposed Noah’s religion, and reads the ‘evil conduct’ as that very plea.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":26,"at":[1648],"ref":"Yusuf 12:53","key":"I do not exculpate myself. Lo! the (human) soul enjoineth unto evil, save that whereon my Lord hath mercy.","s":"Joseph is cleared when the ruler’s wife confesses before the king; Ibn Kathir, on 12:50 to 12:53, reads it as her words, confessing the desire behind her advances, since Joseph was not yet present, and al-Tabari, on 12:53, as Joseph’s own, admitting that souls urge what they desire unless his Lord has mercy.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":27,"at":[1717],"ref":"Ar-Ra'd 13:11","key":"Lo! Allah changeth not the condition of a folk until they (first) change that which is in their hearts; and if Allah willeth misfortune for a folk there is none that can repel it, nor have they a defender beside Him.","s":"Amid verses on God’s knowledge of the hidden and the open, the surah speaks of a people’s lot; al-Tabari, on 13:11, takes it as the well-being and favour God does not strip away in punishment until they wrong and transgress against one another.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":28,"at":[1734],"ref":"Ar-Ra'd 13:28","key":"Verily in the remembrance of Allah do hearts find rest!","s":"Answering the call for a sign, the surah describes those God guides; Ibn Kathir, on 13:27 to 13:29, takes such rest as hearts leaning towards God, content with Him as protector and helper, and this line as saying He is worthy of it.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":29,"at":[1810],"ref":"Al-Hijr 15:9","key":"Lo! We, even We, reveal the Reminder, and lo! We verily are its Guardian.","s":"Answering those who call the Prophet mad, the surah turns to the revelation itself; Ibn Kathir, on 15:6 to 15:9, takes the Reminder as the Qur’an, guarded by God from alteration and change, and prefers this to taking the Prophet as the one guarded.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":30,"at":[1997],"ref":"An-Nahl 16:97","key":"Whosoever doeth right, whether male or female, and is a believer, him verily we shall quicken with good life, and We shall pay them a recompense in proportion to the best of what they used to do.","s":"After warning against trading God’s covenant for small gain, the surah turns to reward; al-Tabari, on 16:97, takes the good life as contentment, the reading he prefers among several, since one made content with his portion is not troubled chasing what escapes him.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":31,"at":[2029],"ref":"Al-Isra 17:1","key":"Glorified be He Who carried His servant by night from the Inviolable Place of Worship to the Far distant place of worship the neighbourhood whereof We have blessed, that We might show him of Our tokens!","s":"Al-Isra opens by glorifying God for a journey by night; al-Tabari, on 17:1, takes it as bodily, not of the spirit alone, since a journey like a dream would be no sign of prophethood, nor anything its deniers would have disputed.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":32,"at":[2106],"ref":"Al-Isra 17:78","key":"Establish worship at the going down of the sun until the dark of night, and (the recital of) the Qur’an at dawn.","s":"Turning from those who would drive the Prophet out, the surah gives him the hours of prayer; al-Tabari, on 17:78, takes ‘going down’ as the sun’s leaning past midday, for the noon prayer, and nightfall as the sunset prayer.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":33,"at":[2185],"ref":"Al-Kahf 18:46","key":"Wealth and children are an ornament of the life of the world. But the good deeds which endure are better in thy Lord’s sight for reward, and better in respect of hope.","s":"After the ruined garden and the likeness of plants dried to twigs, the ayah weighs passing goods against lasting ones; al-Tabari, on 18:46, takes the enduring deeds as all good works, not only the five prayers or words of praise, since God singled none out.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":34,"at":[2359],"ref":"Ta-Ha 20:12","key":"Lo! I, even I, am thy Lord, So take off thy shoes, for lo! thou art in the holy valley of Tuwa.","s":"Early in Ta-Ha, Moses reaches the fire and is called by name; al-Tabari, on 20:11 to 20:12, reads the command as letting his bare feet touch the blessing of a sanctified valley, since nothing in the text ties it to sandals of donkey skin.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":35,"at":[2471],"ref":"Ta-Ha 20:124","key":"But he who turneth away from remembrance of Me, his will be a narrow life, and I shall bring him blind to the assembly on the Day of Resurrection.","s":"After Adam and his wife are sent down with a promise of guidance, the verse turns to the fate of those who refuse it; al-Tabari, on 20:124 to 20:126, reads the narrow life as the grave’s torment, since many who turn away live in ease.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":36,"at":[2589],"ref":"Al-Anbiya 21:107","key":"We sent thee not save as a mercy for the peoples.","s":"Near the close of Al-Anbiya, after its line of earlier prophets, the Prophet is addressed; al-Tabari, on 21:106 to 21:107, reads the mercy as meant for believer and denier alike, the one guided to the Garden, the other spared the swift ruin of earlier peoples.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":37,"at":[2631],"ref":"Al-Hajj 22:37","key":"Their flesh and their food reach not Allah, but the devotion from you reacheth Him.","s":"After the pilgrims are told to name God over their camels, the verse turns to the offering itself; al-Tabari, on 22:37 to 22:38, reads what reaches Him as their fear of Him in the sacrifice, offered for His sake and as He bade.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":38,"at":[2787],"ref":"Al-Mu'minun 23:115","key":"Deemed ye then that We had created you for naught, and that ye would not be returned unto Us?","s":"After the doomed are told they tarried on earth but a little, God questions them; al-Tabari, on 23:114 to 23:115, takes it as asking the wretched whether they thought themselves made in play and falsehood, not to live again and be repaid for their deeds.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":39,"at":[2825],"ref":"An-Nur 24:35","key":"Allah is the Light of the heavens and the earth. The similitude of His light is as a niche wherein is a lamp.","s":"Following a verse on the plain revelations sent down, the chapter gives its parable of the lamp; al-Tabari, on 24:35, reads the Light as God’s guidance of all in heaven and earth, and the lamp as a parable of the Qur’an in believers’ hearts.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":40,"at":[2926],"ref":"Al-Furqan 25:72","key":"And those who will not witness vanity, but when they pass near senseless play, pass by with dignity.","s":"Near the surah’s close, the portrait of the slaves of the Beneficent turns from night prayer and spending to what they shun; al-Tabari, on 25:72, reads the ‘vanity’ as every falsehood, associating partners with God, singing and lying alike, since the description is general.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":41,"at":[3020],"ref":"Ash-Shu'ara 26:89","key":"Save him who bringeth unto Allah a whole heart.","s":"Abraham’s prayer turns to the day of raising, when wealth and sons avail nothing, and names the one exception; al-Tabari, on 26:85 to 26:89, reads the ‘whole heart’ as one free of doubt about God’s oneness and the raising after death.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":42,"at":[3220],"ref":"An-Naml 27:62","key":"Is not He (best) Who answereth the wronged one when he crieth unto Him and removeth the evil, and hath made you viceroys of the earth?","s":"Among questions setting God against the partners ascribed to Him, this one turns to human need; Ibn Kathir, on 27:62, reads it as naming God the one called on in hardship, to whom alone the desperate turn and who alone lifts harm.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":43,"at":[3277],"ref":"Al-Qasas 28:26","key":"One of the two women said: O my father! Hire him! For the best (man) that thou canst hire in the strong, the trustworthy.","s":"Moses, having watered two women’s flocks, now stands before their aged father; the Jalalayn, on 28:26, explains the strength and trust by what she told her father, his lifting the well’s stone and bidding her walk behind him.","by":"the Jalalayn"},{"d":44,"at":[3384],"ref":"Al-'Ankabut 29:45","key":"Lo! worship preserveth from lewdness and iniquity, but verily remembrance of Allah is more important.","s":"After the spider parable, the Prophet is told to recite the revelation and keep up the prayer; al-Tabari, on 29:44 to 29:45, takes the ‘remembrance of Allah’ as God’s remembrance of His servants, greater than theirs of Him, judging this nearest the plain text.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":45,"at":[3487],"ref":"Luqman 31:19","key":"Be modest in thy bearing and subdue thy voice.","s":"Luqman’s counsel to his son passes from prayer and patience to conduct among people; Ibn Kathir, on 31:16 to 31:19, reads the modest ‘bearing’ as a gait neither sluggish nor hurried, and the subdued voice as speech not raised needlessly.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":46,"at":[3553],"ref":"Al-Ahzab 33:21","key":"Verily in the messenger of Allah ye have a good example for him who looketh unto Allah and the Last Day, and remembereth Allah much.","s":"As the clans lay siege and waverers seek leave, the besieged are pointed to the messenger; al-Tabari, on 33:21 to 33:22, reads it as reproaching believers who hung back: one hoping for God’s reward does not spare himself, but stays with him wherever he is.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":47,"at":[3604],"ref":"Al-Ahzab 33:72","key":"Lo! We offered the trust unto the heavens and the earth and the hills, but they shrank from bearing it and were afraid of it. And man assumed it.","s":"Near the surah’s close, after the believers are told to speak straight, the ayah turns to the burden man took on; al-Tabari, on 33:72, reads the trust as every trust of religion and of people, since God confined it to no one kind.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":48,"at":[3691],"ref":"Fatir 35:32","key":"Then We gave the Scripture as inheritance unto those whom We elected of Our bondmen. But of them are some who wrong themselves and of them are some who are lukewarm, and of them are some who outstrip (others) through good deeds, by Allah’s leave.","s":"After the Prophet’s Scripture is said to confirm what came before, the ayah sorts its heirs into three; al-Tabari, on 35:32, reads the one who wrongs himself as guilty of sins short of hypocrisy and idolatry, since the Gardens promised next include all three.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":49,"at":[3782],"ref":"Ya-Sin 36:78","key":"And he hath coined for Us a similitude, and hath forgotten the fact of his creation, saying: Who will revive these bones when they have rotted away?","s":"After man, made from a drop, is called an open opponent, the ayah gives his challenge; Ibn Kathir, on 36:77 to 36:80, reads it, whether Ubayy ibn Khalaf or al-ʿAs ibn Wa’il occasioned it, as meant for every denier of the resurrection.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":50,"at":[3889],"ref":"As-Saffat 37:102","key":"And when (his son) was old enough to walk with him, (Abraham) said: O my dear son, I have seen in a dream that I must sacrifice thee. So look, what thinkest thou?","s":"After the tidings of a gentle son, the ayah brings father and son to the trial; al-Tabari, on 37:101 to 37:102, reads the father’s question as sounding the son’s resolve, not seeking counsel on obeying God, whose command he would carry out in any case.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":51,"at":[4110],"ref":"Az-Zumar 39:53","key":"Say: O My slaves who have been prodigal to their own hurt! Despair not of the mercy of Allah, Who forgiveth all sins.","s":"After the warning that a boon granted is a test, the Prophet is given words for those deep in sin; al-Tabari, on 39:53, reads them as meant for every such sinner, believer or idolater, though idolatry is forgiven only upon repentance.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":52,"at":[4160],"ref":"Ghafir 40:28","key":"If he is lying, then his lie is upon him; and if he is truthful, then some of that wherewith he threateneth you will strike you.","s":"As Pharaoh proposes to kill Moses, a believer of his own house who hides his faith speaks up; al-Qurtubi, on 40:28, reads the concession as no doubt of Moses’ truth but a gentle way to hold the people back from harming him.","by":"al-Qurtubi"},{"d":53,"at":[4251],"ref":"Fussilat 41:34","key":"The good deed and the evil deed are not alike. Repel the evil deed with one which is better, then lo! he, between whom and thee there was enmity (will become) as though he was a bosom friend.","s":"After praise for one who prays and does right, the Prophet is taught how to answer enmity; al-Tabari, on 41:33 to 41:34, reads it as repelling ignorance with forbearance, offence with pardon and harm with patience, till the foe is like a near kinsman.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":54,"at":[4391],"ref":"Az-Zukhruf 43:67","key":"Friends on that day will be foes one to another, save those who kept their duty (to Allah).","s":"Between the sudden Hour and the welcome into the Garden, the surah speaks of friendship; Ibn Kathir, on 43:66 to 43:73, reads it as every friendship not for God turning to enmity at the Resurrection, while one for God lasts as He lasts.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":55,"at":[4442],"ref":"Ad-Dukhan 44:29","key":"And the heaven and the earth wept not for them, nor were they reprieved.","s":"Pharaoh’s folk drowned and their gardens left to others, the surah closes their story; al-Tabari, on 44:29 to 44:31, explains, from reports, that heaven and earth weep for a dying believer, but these had no good work rising to God and no place of prayer.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":56,"at":[4524],"ref":"Al-Ahqaf 46:15","key":"And We have commended unto man kindness toward parents. His mother beareth him with reluctance, and bringeth him forth with reluctance, and the bearing of him and the weaning of him is thirty months, till, when he attaineth full strength and reacheth forty years, he saith: My Lord!","s":"Against a son who scorns his parents, the surah sets a life from womb to forty; al-Tabari, on 46:15, reads forty as the age when God’s proof against a man is complete: youth’s folly gone, he knows what he owes God in honouring his parents.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":57,"at":[4624],"ref":"Al-Hujurat 49:13","key":"Lo! We have created you male and female, and have made you nations and tribes that ye may know one another. Lo! the noblest of you, in the sight of Allah, is the best in conduct.","s":"After forbidding believers to mock, spy and backbite, the surah addresses all humankind; al-Tabari, on 49:13, reads the tribes as made for knowing near and distant kin, not as merit before God, and the noblest as the most God-fearing, not the grandest house.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":58,"at":[4730],"ref":"Adh-Dhariyat 51:56","key":"I created the jinn and humankind only that they might worship Me.","s":"Having told the Prophet to turn away yet warn, God speaks in the first person; al-Tabari, on 51:56 to 51:57, takes them, with Ibn Abbas, as made to worship and bow to Him, since deniers too yield to His decree, resisting only His bidding.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":59,"at":[4929],"ref":"Ar-Rahman 55:29","key":"All that are in the heavens and the earth entreat Him. Every day He exerciseth (universal) power.","s":"Between the passing of all on earth and a warning to jinn and men, the surah turns to prayer; Ibn Kathir, on 55:26 to 55:30, reads it as God needing none and all creatures needing Him every moment, asking by their state and their speech.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":60,"at":[5110],"ref":"Al-Mujadilah 58:7","key":"There is no secret conference of three but He is their fourth, nor of five but He is their sixth, nor of less than that or more but He is with them wheresoever they may be; and afterward, on the Day of Resurrection, He will inform them of what they did.","s":"Between a wife’s complaint heard by God and a warning to those who conspire against the messenger, the Prophet is asked to consider God’s knowledge; al-Tabari, on 58:7, reads ‘their fourth’ as God witnessing them by His knowledge while He is on His Throne.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":61,"at":[5143],"ref":"Al-Hashr 59:18","key":"And let every soul look to that which it sendeth on before for the morrow.","s":"After the hypocrites are likened to the devil, the believers are twice told to keep their duty to God; Ibn Kathir, on 59:18 to 59:20, reads the verse as a call to reckon with oneself before being reckoned with.","by":"Ibn Kathir"},{"d":62,"at":[5214],"ref":"At-Taghabun 64:16","key":"So keep your duty to Allah as best ye can, and listen, and obey, and spend; that is better for your souls.","s":"After wealth and children are called a trial, the believers are given their charge; al-Tabari, on 64:15 to 64:16, takes it as not cancelling the earlier command to fear God as is His due, for nothing clear says so, and keeps both in force.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":63,"at":[5428],"ref":"Nuh 71:10","key":"And I have said: Seek pardon of your Lord. Lo! He was ever Forgiving.","s":"Noah, telling God how his people stopped their ears to his calling, recalls what he urged on them; al-Qurtubi, on 71:10 to 71:12, takes the passage as evidence that seeking forgiveness brings down rain and provision, when it comes with sincerity and leaving sin.","by":"al-Qurtubi"},{"d":64,"at":[5566],"ref":"Al-Qiyamah 75:16","key":"Stir not thy tongue herewith to hasten it.","s":"In a chapter on the Day of Resurrection, an aside turns to the Prophet receiving revelation; al-Tabari, on 75:16 to 75:19, takes it as barring haste to repeat the words before God had gathered them in his breast, not later rehearsal to remember them.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":65,"at":[5861],"ref":"Al-Mutaffifin 83:14","key":"Nay, but that which they have earned is rust upon their hearts.","s":"Against deniers who dismiss the revelations as old fables, the chapter gives its answer; al-Tabari, on 83:12 to 83:14, reads the rust as sins that overpower the heart, flood it and close round it until they cover it, as wine overpowers the mind.","by":"al-Tabari"},{"d":66,"at":[6127],"ref":"Al-Qadr 97:3","key":"The Night of Power is better than a thousand months.","s":"In a surah on the night of revelation, between the sending down and the angels’ descent, the ayah weighs the night’s worth; al-Tabari, on 97:1 to 97:5, reads it as deeds done that night outweighing the deeds of a thousand months that lack it.","by":"al-Tabari"}]});
